Title: "Quotients and Actions"
Id: "442c4a8a-b530-4fe4-8973-1f339026c805"


Text: "While subgroups isolate the internal substructures of a group, *quotient
       groups* perform the dual geometric task: collapsing indistinguishable
       elements to reveal large-scale structure.

       However, forming a quotient group requires more than an arbitrary
       subgroup: coset multiplication $(gH)(hH) = (gh)H$ is well-defined if and
       only if the subgroup is *normal* ($gHg^{-1} = H$ for all $g$). Group
       actions provide the external, dynamical counterpart to this internal
       architecture: an abstract group is realized as a group of transformations
       acting on a geometric set. The concepts of kernel, normal subgroup,
       orbit, and stabilizer interweave these perspectives into a unified theory
       of mathematical symmetry."
Id: "414988da-2898-41e8-8f3f-74753d59eff1"


SectionTitle: "Cosets and Normal Subgroups"
Id: "7c8c60ce-9c18-4ea7-8447-9b47c9118f8b"


[\left.coset:of{H}:by{g}:in{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Defines: C := \set@{g * h_ : h_ "in" H}
when:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
. g "in" G
Documented:
. called: "left coset of $H?$ by $g?$"
. written: "g?H?"
Id: "b0bd6ac9-937e-46cb-b2f0-9752c5d2a6e0"


[\right.coset:of{H}:by{g}:in{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Defines: C := \set@{h_ * g : h_ "in" H}
when:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
. g "in" G
Documented:
. called: "right coset of $H?$ by $g?$"
. written: "H?g?"
Id: "152ef2a9-044c-4687-8325-bc80ce2bfa12"


Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
. g1, g2 "in" G
then:
. oneOf:
  . \left.coset:of{H}:by{g1}:in{G} = \left.coset:of{H}:by{g2}:in{G}
  . \left.coset:of{H}:by{g1}:in{G} \.disjoint.from./ \left.coset:of{H}:by{g2}:in{G}
Documented:
. called: "Left Cosets are Equal or Disjoint"
Proof: """Let $g_1, g_2 \in G$. Suppose that the left cosets $g_1H$ and $g_2H$
          are not disjoint. Then there exists at least one element
          $x \in g_1H \cap g_2H$. By definition of left cosets, there exist
          elements $h_1, h_2 \in H$ such that: $$ x = g_1 * h_1 = g_2 * h_2. $$

          Multiplying both sides on the right by $h_1^{-1}$ gives:
          $$ g_1 = g_2 * (h_2 * h_1^{-1}). $$ Since $H$ is a subgroup and
          $h_1, h_2 \in H$, the element $h_3 = h_2 * h_1^{-1}$ belongs to $H$.

          Now let $y \in g_1H$ be an arbitrary element of $g_1H$. Then
          $y = g_1 * h$ for some $h \in H$. Substituting our expression for
          $g_1$: $$ y = (g_2 * h_3) * h = g_2 * (h_3 * h). $$ Since $H$ is
          closed under multiplication, $h_3 * h \in H$, which proves that
          $y \in g_2H$. Therefore, $g_1H \subseteq g_2H$.

          By an entirely symmetric argument exchanging the indices $1$ and $2$,
          we obtain $g_2H \subseteq g_1H$. Consequently, $g_1H = g_2H$. Thus,
          any two left cosets are either identical or disjoint, partitioning the
          group $G$."""
Id: "4cafbd92-cf4d-4989-ac8f-e53ed45cb238"


[\normal.subgroup:of{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Declares: N is \group
when: G is \group
satisfies:
. N \:subgroup?:/ G
. forAll:
  . g "in" G
  . n "in" G
  where: n "in"? N
  then:
  . exists: m "in" G
    suchThat:
    . m "in"? N
    . g * n = m * g
Enables:
. capability: N_ <| G_ :=> N_ is \normal.subgroup:of{G_}
Documented:
. called: "normal subgroup of $G?$"
. written: "H? \vartriangleleft G?"
. description: "A subgroup is normal when its left and right cosets agree for
                every group element."
Id: "0e5638e1-1109-4980-a652-2e795bed2e44"


TextTheorem: "Let $N \trianglelefteq G$ be a normal subgroup. The set of left
              cosets $G/N = \{gN : g \in G\}$ forms a group under the operation
              $(gN)(hN) = (gh)N$, called the *quotient group* (or factor group)
              of $G$ by $N$.

              *Proof of Well-Definedness*: Suppose $g_1N = g_2N$ and
              $h_1N = h_2N$. Then $g_2 = g_1 n_1$ and $h_2 = h_1 n_2$ for some
              $n_1, n_2 \in N$. Then:
              $$ g_2 h_2 = (g_1 n_1)(h_1 n_2) = g_1 (n_1 h_1) n_2. $$ Since $N$
              is normal, $n_1 h_1 = h_1 n_3$ for some $n_3 \in N$. Substituting
              gives $g_2 h_2 = g_1 (h_1 n_3) n_2 = (g_1 h_1)(n_3 n_2)$. Since
              $n_3 n_2 \in N$, we have $(g_2 h_2)N = (g_1 h_1)N$. Thus
              multiplication is independent of the chosen representatives.

              The identity of $G/N$ is $eN = N$, and the inverse of $gN$ is
              $g^{-1}N$. If $N$ is not normal, coset multiplication depends on
              representative choices and fails to define an operation on $G/N$."
Id: "1c5bfe13-52ac-46ae-b75c-af1d8db93224"


[\group.kernel:of{phi(x__)}:from{G1 ::= (X1, *_1, e1)}:to{G2 ::= (X2, *_2, e2)}]
Defines: K := \set@{g_ : g_ "in" G1 | phi(g_) = e2}
when:
. G1, G2 is \group
. phi is \group.homomorphism:from{G1}:to{G2}
Documented:
. called: "kernel of $phi?$"
. written: "\ker(phi?)"
Id: "dbdd8045-535e-4d8b-b4d1-2f10e2d2648f"


[\group.image:of{phi(x__)}:from{G1 ::= (X1, *_1, e1)}:to{G2 ::= (X2, *_2, e2)}]
Defines: I := \set@{phi(g_) : g_ "in" G1}
when:
. G1, G2 is \group
. phi is \group.homomorphism:from{G1}:to{G2}
Documented:
. called: "image of $phi?$"
. written: "\operatorname{im}(phi?)"
Id: "f13b0e2b-be95-4735-b963-fbc91db6b175"


TextTheorem: "The First Isomorphism Theorem for Groups. Let $\varphi : G \to H$ be a
              group homomorphism. Then:
              1. The kernel $\ker(\varphi)$ is a normal subgroup of $G$: $\ker(\varphi) \trianglelefteq G$.
              2. The image $\operatorname{im}(\varphi)$ is a subgroup of $H$.
              3. The map $\widetilde{\varphi} : G / \ker(\varphi) \to \operatorname{im}(\varphi)$
                 defined by $\widetilde{\varphi}(g\ker(\varphi)) = \varphi(g)$ is a well-defined
                 group isomorphism:
                 $$ G / \ker(\varphi) \cong \operatorname{im}(\varphi). $$
                 
              *Proof*:
              - Normality: If $k \in \ker(\varphi)$ and $g \in G$, then
                $\varphi(g * k * g^{-1}) = \varphi(g) * \varphi(k) * \varphi(g)^{-1} = \varphi(g) * e_H * \varphi(g)^{-1} = e_H$,
                so $g * k * g^{-1} \in \ker(\varphi)$.
              - Well-Defined and Injective: $g_1 \ker(\varphi) = g_2 \ker(\varphi) \iff g_1^{-1} g_2 \in \ker(\varphi) \iff \varphi(g_1^{-1} g_2) = e_H \iff \varphi(g_1) = \varphi(g_2)$.
                Reading left-to-right gives well-definedness; right-to-left gives injectivity.
              - Homomorphism and Surjectivity: Multiplicativity of $\varphi$ implies
                $\widetilde{\varphi}((g_1 N)(g_2 N)) = \varphi(g_1 g_2) = \varphi(g_1)\varphi(g_2) = \widetilde{\varphi}(g_1 N)\widetilde{\varphi}(g_2 N)$,
                and surjectivity onto $\operatorname{im}(\varphi)$ is immediate.
                
              Thus every homomorphic image is canonically identifiable with a quotient group."
Id: "033fb304-f310-43fa-84fb-2070d17de0e2"


SectionTitle: "Group Actions"
Id: "3033f57e-9ff5-4da7-bf72-b3c09c27f533"


[\group.action:of{G ::= (Y, *, e)}:on{X}]
Declares: alpha(g_, x_) is \function:on{Y \.set.cross./ X}:to{X}
when:
. G is \group
. X is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" X
  then: alpha(e, x) = x
. forAll:
  . g, h "in" G
  . x "in" X
  then: alpha(g * h, x) = alpha(g, alpha(h, x))
Documented:
. called: "group action of $G?$ on $X?$"
. writing: alpha(g_, x_)
  as: "g_? \cdot x_?"
. description: "A left action represents each group element as a symmetry of the
                set and respects identity and multiplication."
Id: "fa043cd6-0ce8-4504-82bb-aba9ad2c8a30"


[\orbit:of{x}:under{alpha}:of{G}:on{X}]
Defines: O := \set@{alpha(g_, x) : g_ "in" G}
when:
. G is \group
. X is \set
. alpha is \group.action:of{G}:on{X}
. x "in" X
Documented:
. called: "orbit of $x?$ under $alpha?$"
. written: "G? \cdot x?"
Id: "373a9fce-21a7-4155-9f1d-cfb662941906"


[\stabilizer:of{x}:under{alpha}:of{G}:on{X}]
Defines: S := \set@{g_ : g_ "in" G | alpha(g_, x) = x}
when:
. G is \group
. X is \set
. alpha is \group.action:of{G}:on{X}
. x "in" X
Documented:
. called: "stabilizer of $x?$ under $alpha?$"
. written: "G?_{x?}"
Id: "02a3fe2e-dbbf-49a6-8cf2-cc5cc85568f7"


TextTheorem: "The Orbit-Stabilizer Theorem. Let a group $G$ act on a set $X$ via $\alpha$.
              For any element $x \in X$, there is a natural bijection between the left coset
              space $G / G_x$ and the orbit $G \cdot x$, given by:
              $$ \Phi : G / G_x \longrightarrow G \cdot x, \quad \Phi(g G_x) = g \cdot x. $$
              Consequently, if $G$ is finite:
              $$ |G| = |G \cdot x| \cdot |G_x| = [G : G_x] \cdot |G_x|. $$
              
              *Proof*:
              - Well-Definedness and Injectivity: For $g, h \in G$:
                $$ g \cdot x = h \cdot x \iff h^{-1} \cdot (g \cdot x) = x \iff (h^{-1} * g) \cdot x = x \iff h^{-1} * g \in G_x \iff g G_x = h G_x. $$
                Reading left-to-right proves injectivity; reading right-to-left proves well-definedness.
              - Surjectivity: By definition of the orbit, every $y \in G \cdot x$ is of the form
                $y = g \cdot x = \Phi(g G_x)$ for some $g \in G$.
              Thus $\Phi$ is a well-defined bijection."
Id: "8b9d1618-6fe0-4cd1-a338-3eb2e3242b9a"


Text: "Group actions translate abstract algebraic structure into concrete
       combinatorics and geometry.

       A classic instance is the *conjugation action* of a group $G$ on itself:
       $g \cdot x = g * x * g^{-1}$. In this setting:
       - The orbit of $x$ is the *conjugacy class* $\operatorname{Cl}(x)$.
       - The stabilizer of $x$ is the *centralizer*
         $C_G(x) = \{g \in G : g * x = x * g\}$.
       - The fixed points of the action form the center $Z(G)$.

       Applying the Orbit-Stabilizer theorem to this action yields the
       celebrated *Class Equation* for a finite group:
       $$ |G| = |Z(G)| + \sum_{i=1}^k [G : C_G(x_i)], $$ where $x_1, \dots, x_k$
       are representatives of the non-central conjugacy classes. The Class
       Equation is a cornerstone of finite group theory, proving for instance
       that every non-trivial finite $p$-group has a non-trivial center, and
       opening the gateway to the Sylow theorems."
Id: "f873aaf6-708d-4dc9-a895-0a82e2ca658c"
